  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nPositively tilted H5 trough is present across portions of the \nnorthern Plains and lower Missouri River Valley with two distinct \nvort maxima. Both of these have provided enough lift and dCVA to \ndeepen a surface cyclone across much of the Central Plains. Surface \npressure falls have extended eastward into the lower Missouri River \nValley. Weak thermal boundary has aided in the development of \norganized convection, along with enough deep layer shear that is \npresent. Instability has been weakening during the overnight hours but \nthere may still be enough to keep storm activity going through 09-\n10z timeframe, then should start to see any organized activity break \napart. A few sporadic wind gusts to around 60-70 MPH may remain \npossible before sunrise, as we have seen some organization when the \nline became oriented perpendicular to the 0-3km bulk shear vector. \nThe first vort maxima and instability axis should exit the forecast \narea to the east between 15-16z late this morning. Second vort \nmaxima will start moving across the eastern Plains this afternoon, \nwith another shot of dCVA reinforcing surface pressure falls across \nthe region and development of surface cyclone. A stronger cold front \nwill develop with this surface feature and track eastward across the \narea through this afternoon and evening. Depending on how long \nconvective debris lingers from the morning, we may see further \ndestabilization east of Interstate 35 which may allow for stronger \nstorms to develop, and perhaps isolated severe storms with 60-70 MPH \nwinds and a few hail stones that could be around quarter sized or \nslightly larger. As the front pushes eastward, this activity should \nexit the forecast area after 01z if it develops in central Missouri \nthis afternoon and evening. With respect to temperatures today, the \ncloud cover and morning convective complex adds some uncertainty, \nbut the continued WAA should allow temperatures to easily hit the \nupper 80s again, with some lower 90s possible especially south of US \nHighway 50. During the early afternoon, dewpoint temperatures will \nprobably hit the mid 70s for areas south. Our southern zones may see \nan hour or two of heat index values right around 105F. However, \ngiven the current radar and satellite trends, confidence was not \nhigh enough to issue a Heat Advisory at this time, but we will \ncontinue to monitor temperature and dewpoint trends. If there is \nrapid clearing on the backside of exiting convection, a heat \nheadline may become needed south of Interstate 70. \n\nThe cold front passage will bring relief to the heat and humidity \nacross the area through the weekend, with high temperatures in the \nlower to mid 80s, and dewpoint temperatures in the lower to mid 60s. \nThat northwesterly flow will end Sunday evening. Amplified mid-\nlevel ridge then builds over the western CONUS but pushes H5 height \nrises into the Central CONUS through the middle of next week. Low-\nlevel flow will turn more southerly which will begin to push \ntemperatures back into the lower 90s by the middle of the next week. \nForecast will remain mostly dry, with slight chances entering the \nforecast into Wednesday and Thursday next week as a disturbance \nattempts to drop southeastward from the Pacific Northwest.",
